From 0c6763424e791fab07d592ab9c759ac2ccc704e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jky72 <jky72@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 09:11:37 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] feat(input): implement the kitty keyboard protocol
 encoder

pi requires the kitty keyboard protocol (its docs: "Pi uses the Kitty keyboard
protocol for reliable modifier key detection") and pushes `CSI > 7 u` at startup,
which par-term could not honour: the core already tracks, queries and answers the
flag stack, but nothing on the encode side read those flags, so the handler only ever
produced modifyOtherKeys and legacy sequences.

Add the encoder (`try_kitty_encoding`) with the per-flag rules for `disambiguate`,
`report_all_keys`, `report_alternate_keys` and `report_event_types`, including the
rule that a printable key is sent as its unshifted base codepoint with Shift
expressed as a modifier bit. Wire the flags through TerminalLike (real type plus the
test double), cache them per tab the way the existing mode caches are, and read them
in the key handler ahead of the Shift+Enter path so kitty wins when it is active.

Two defects found while verifying against pi's real flags (7, i.e. report_all off):

* Space was encoded as CSI-u instead of a plain space, so typed text arrived with the
  spaces missing. Plain characters, space, Enter, Tab and Backspace must stay literal
  bytes for this flag set; only `report_all_keys` turns ordinary characters into
  CSI-u, and Enter must keep the default event type for ConPTY compatibility.
* The encoding path is an opt-in push/pop protocol, so no code path may assume it is
  active: with flags at zero the encoder returns None and the existing
  modifyOtherKeys -> legacy fallback runs unchanged.

Add `input.kitty_keyboard` (default true) as an escape hatch for applications that
request the protocol but cannot parse the result, and regression tests covering the
flag combinations pi actually uses.

Note: reading the flags is also what emits the `KITTY_KEYBOARD flags requested:` log
line, which only appears on a key event - do not wait for it as a startup signal.
